From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] wmvcore: IWMWriter profile, inputs and state

IWMWriter had no state at all: every call but the sink list and the sync
tolerance was a stub returning E_NOTIMPL.

- SetProfile takes a private copy of any profile object (its streams, lists
  and storage format), one input per stream. SetOutputFilename stores the
  file name.
- GetInputCount, GetInputFormatCount, GetInputFormat, GetInputProps and
  SetInputProps: the formats an input accepts are the uncompressed ones for
  the profile's stream: PCM (16 bit, the stream's channels and rate) for an
  audio stream, I420/YV12/YUY2/RGB24/RGB32 frames of the stream's size for a
  video stream, the two-strings command type for a script stream, none for
  other types. GetInputProps returns the first format until SetInputProps
  chose one; SetInputProps refuses another major type, another sample rate or
  frame size (NS_E_INVALID_INPUT_FORMAT). IWMInputMediaProps carries the
  media type and the connection name of the stream. A writer without a
  profile answers NS_E_INVALIDPROFILE; indices out of range E_INVALIDARG.
- BeginWriting checks the profile (NS_E_INVALIDPROFILE), an output (a file
  name or a sink, NS_E_NOT_CONFIGURED) and that every input with formats has
  one chosen (NS_E_INVALID_INPUT_FORMAT). Producing the ASF stream needs the
  encoders of the profile's streams, which are not available: it then returns
  E_NOTIMPL, so writing never starts; EndWriting, WriteSample, Flush and
  IWMWriterAdvanced::WriteStreamSample answer NS_E_INVALID_REQUEST (not
  writing) after their argument checks. AllocateSample returns a sample buffer.
- SetProfileByID stays E_NOTIMPL (no system profiles) after its argument check.

